# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  FixControl cluster agent — THE ONE FILE YOU EDIT.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   cp values.example.env values.env && chmod 600 values.env
#   $EDITOR values.env
#   ./install.sh
#
# Everything the install needs comes from here. There is no second place to
# look and no flag that changes behaviour behind this file's back: `install.sh`
# renders a complete, readable kustomize overlay from these values into
# `install/.render/`, prints what it will apply, and applies it. Re-running it
# with the same file is a no-op; re-running it with a changed file rolls
# exactly what changed.
#
# `values.env` is gitignored. It holds secrets. Keep it 0600, or set the
# *_FILE variants below and keep the material somewhere your platform already
# protects — or set FC_MANAGE_SECRETS=0 and let External Secrets / Vault own
# the Kubernetes Secrets entirely (see "Secrets" below).
#
# Values are plain shell assignments. Quote anything containing spaces, commas
# are fine unquoted.


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  1. FixControl connection and identity
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Where this cluster dials OUT to. This is the ONLY address FixControl needs;
# nothing dials in. A plaintext http:// URL is refused unless
# FC_ALLOW_INSECURE_URL=1, which exists for dev rigs and nothing else.
FC_URL=https://api.fixcontrol.ai

# From FixControl → /settings/devops. The tenant this agent is bound to and the
# cluster id it claims. Check 1 (tenant_mismatch) and check 2 (cluster_mismatch)
# are unenforceable without them, so the agent refuses to start if they are
# missing.
FC_TENANT=
FC_CLUSTER_ID=

# ── The rollout/CI agent's identity ────────────────────────────────────────
# `agentId` from enrolment, and the secret FixControl shows exactly ONCE.
# Rotation: see ./rotate-agent-secret.sh — never edit the secret in the cluster
# by hand.
FC_AGENT_ID=
FC_AGENT_SECRET=
# …or keep the secret out of this file entirely:
#FC_AGENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/fc-agent-secret

# ── The test runner's identity — a SEPARATE registration ───────────────────
# Deliberately not the same credential. fc-test-runner executes customer test
# plans; fc-rollout-controller advances production rollouts. One compromise
# must not be the other. Leave blank (and FC_INSTALL_TEST_RUNNER=0) if you are
# not running sandbox environments in this cluster.
FC_TEST_RUNNER_AGENT_ID=
FC_TEST_RUNNER_AGENT_SECRET=
#FC_TEST_RUNNER_AGENT_SECRET_FILE=

# ── The FixControl signing keys this cluster TRUSTS (check 3) ──────────────
# Ask a FixControl platform operator:  npx tsx scripts/agent-signing-key.ts export
# One key normally; two during a rotation window; never more. PUBLIC material —
# these keys verify and cannot sign, which is why they live in a ConfigMap.
#
# Leaving this empty keeps the LEGACY shared-secret verification, in which the
# key that authorizes this pod is a key this pod holds. That is a migration
# step, not a posture. FC_REQUIRE_SIGNING_PIN=1 makes install.sh refuse to
# install without a pin; leave it at 1 for any production cluster.
FC_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEYS=
FC_REQUIRE_SIGNING_PIN=1


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  2. What to install, and where
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# A dedicated namespace for the agent is the recommended shape: it lets the
# default-deny NetworkPolicy below be unconditionally correct, and it keeps the
# agent out of the namespaces it acts on. It does NOT have to be a namespace
# that contains Rollouts — see FC_ROLLOUT_NAMESPACES.
FC_AGENT_NAMESPACE=fixcontrol-agent
FC_TEST_RUNNER_NAMESPACE=fc-test-runner

FC_INSTALL_AGENT=1
FC_INSTALL_TEST_RUNNER=1

# The ValidatingAdmissionPolicy that pins fc-test-runner's writes to
# `fc-test-*` namespaces. On by default; install.sh skips it with a warning on
# clusters older than 1.30, where VAP is not GA. See base/admission for the
# full argument.
FC_APPLY_ADMISSION_POLICY=1

# Apply a default-deny NetworkPolicy to the agent's namespace. Correct — and
# strongly recommended — when the namespace is dedicated. Set to 0 if you are
# installing into a namespace that already runs your own workloads, and make
# sure your own baseline policy covers it instead.
FC_APPLY_DEFAULT_DENY=1


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  3. Images — digest-pinned, never a tag
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Produce these with:  VERSION=1.0.0 REGISTRY=<your registry> ./scripts/build-images.sh
# which writes build/image-pins.env containing exactly the two lines below,
# ready to paste. The update flow is in the README, Step 17 → "Updating the
# pinned images".
#
# install.sh REFUSES a tag-only reference: a tag is a name that can be
# repointed, and an agent holding three credentials is not a workload where
# "whatever :1.0.0 means today" is an acceptable answer.
FC_AGENT_IMAGE=
FC_TEST_RUNNER_IMAGE=

# Escape hatch for a local kind rig, where images are side-loaded rather than
# pulled. Never set this on a real cluster.
FC_ALLOW_TAG_IMAGES=0

# Name of an existing imagePullSecret in the install namespaces, if your
# registry needs one.
FC_IMAGE_PULL_SECRET=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  4. The customer-owned allowlists (check 6)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# FixControl never sends these. A FixControl-side bug or compromise that emits
# "promote everything in kube-system" is refused right here, by your own
# configuration. Empty allowlists NOTHING — there is deliberately no spelling
# for "all namespaces", and install.sh refuses an empty value rather than
# installing an agent that can never act.

FC_ALLOWED_NAMESPACES=
# "ns/name" items, or "*" for every Rollout inside the namespaces above.
FC_ALLOWED_ROLLOUTS=*

# Where the fc-rollout-controller Role + RoleBinding are created. Defaults to
# FC_ALLOWED_NAMESPACES. Set it explicitly only if the agent must be able to
# READ Rollouts somewhere it is not allowed to ACT — a rare, deliberate shape.
FC_ROLLOUT_NAMESPACES=

# Capabilities this agent CLAIMS. Claiming one is a decision; the default is
# the smallest useful set. Add the CI pair together with FC_CI_CONFIG_FILE.
#   rollout.promote,rollout.abort,rollout.status,ci.approve_deployment,ci.reject_deployment
FC_AGENT_CAPABILITIES=rollout.promote,rollout.status


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  5. How a promotion is actually performed
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#   git      (default, highest security) — a marker commit in your manifests
#            repo; Argo CD discovers it. Every promotion is a reviewable,
#            revertable commit in your own history.
#   receiver — forward the signed envelope to an fc-receiver you already run,
#            over ClusterIP. The migration path for a reverse_webhook install
#            that wants to delete its Ingress.
#   none     — this agent has NO promotion path. The right answer for a
#            CI/CD-gate-only install: the agent carries approve/reject verdicts
#            to your private GitLab/Jenkins and never touches a Rollout.
#            Refused if FC_AGENT_CAPABILITIES claims rollout.promote.
FC_PROMOTE_MODE=git

# PROMOTE_MODE=git
FC_GIT_PROMOTE_REPO=
FC_GIT_PROMOTE_BRANCH=main
# A PAT with contents:write on that repo, and nothing else.
FC_GIT_CREDENTIAL=
#FC_GIT_CREDENTIAL_FILE=

# PROMOTE_MODE=receiver
FC_RECEIVER_URL=
FC_RECEIVER_SECRET=
#FC_RECEIVER_SECRET_FILE=

# The live Argo API. OFF by default. Turning it on is an explicit per-cluster
# decision that ALSO widens RBAC: install.sh adds `rollouts/status: patch` to
# the Role only when this is 1. While it is 0, a `rollout.abort` that would
# need the local API is refused `capability_denied` by the agent AND would be
# refused by RBAC if the agent were compromised into trying.
FC_ARGO_LOCAL_API=0

# Cluster → FixControl events on the same outbound channel. Needs the
# per-tenant Kubernetes webhook secret (the one the connect call returned).
FC_EVENTS_ENABLED=1
FC_K8S_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#FC_K8S_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FILE=

FC_POLL_SECONDS=10


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  6. Private CI hosts (Phase 3) — the customer owns the address
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# A signed CI operation names a PROVIDER and a canonical run id, and NOTHING
# else — no base URL, no hostname, no token. The agent dials only what this
# file points at. A FixControl-side bug that wanted to aim the agent at
# http://169.254.169.254/ has no field to put that address in.
#
# Point this at a JSON file (see ci-config.example.json). Leave it empty and
# the agent allowlists no CI host at all.
FC_CI_CONFIG_FILE=

# The credentials those hosts require. A SEPARATE Kubernetes Secret from the
# FixControl identity, on purpose: different owners, different rotation
# schedules. `credential_env` in the JSON names which variable an entry uses.
FC_GITLAB_TOKEN=
FC_JENKINS_CREDENTIAL=
#FC_GITLAB_TOKEN_FILE=
#FC_JENKINS_CREDENTIAL_FILE=

# A PEM bundle for a CI host with an internal CA. Mounted read-only; the agent
# has no "skip TLS verification" knob, because an agent that can be told to
# ignore TLS is an agent whose CI credential can be harvested by anyone on the
# path.
FC_CI_CA_FILE=

FC_CI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  7. Egress — every destination named, none inferred
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The NetworkPolicy and the allowlists above are two INDEPENDENT statements of
# "which machines may receive a write from this pod". Either alone stops a
# mistake; both together mean a ConfigMap edit that adds a host still cannot
# reach it until a platform engineer also opens the path.
#
# Two spellings, comma-separated, mix freely:
#
#   <cidr>:<port>                      → an ipBlock rule
#                                        e.g. 203.0.113.10/32:443
#   <namespace>|<key>=<value>:<port>   → namespaceSelector + podSelector in ONE
#                                        rule (the two selectors must not be
#                                        split — as separate items they would
#                                        mean "any pod in that namespace OR any
#                                        matching pod anywhere")
#                                        e.g. "argocd|app=fc-gitserver:9418"
#                                        QUOTE IT — an unquoted `|` is a shell
#                                        pipe, and this file is sourced.
#
# Cluster DNS and the kube-apiserver are always included and need no entry.
# There is deliberately no way to spell 0.0.0.0/0 here: this pod holds the
# tenant's agent secret, your manifests-repo PAT and your CI credentials, and a
# broad egress rule turns any code execution in it into arbitrary reach with
# three credentials attached.

# FixControl itself. Resolve api.fixcontrol.ai, or point at your egress proxy.
FC_EGRESS_FIXCONTROL=

# The git forge, for PROMOTE_MODE=git.
FC_EGRESS_GIT=

# Your private GitLab / Jenkins, for the CI capabilities. One entry per host.
FC_EGRESS_CI=

# Anything else this agent must reach. Kept separate so the three above stay
# readable as "who am I governed by", "where do I commit", "what do I release".
FC_EGRESS_EXTRA=

# The test runner reaches FixControl and the API server, and nothing else.
# Defaults to FC_EGRESS_FIXCONTROL.
#
# NOT a destination for this pod: your git host. With the runner-side checkout
# lane enabled (install/base/test-runner/customer-git.example.yaml), the pod
# that clones is the run Job's checkout initContainer in an ephemeral
# `fc-test-*` namespace, not this Deployment. If you run a default-deny
# baseline over those namespaces, open your git host there — see the note at
# the bottom of install/base/test-runner/networkpolicy.yaml.
FC_EGRESS_TEST_RUNNER=

# Autodetected from the `kubernetes` Service in `default` if left empty.
FC_KUBE_APISERVER_CIDR=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  8. Sandbox test runs
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Your image allowlist, e.g. "registry.internal/,docker.io/library/node". It
# covers the toolchain image the plan runs in AND every service image in the
# environment spec (database, cache, sidecars) — both are pulled and run in
# your cluster, so allowlisting one and not the other allowlists nothing.
#
# Empty accepts ANY image FixControl sends. That is the shipped default so an
# existing install keeps working, and it is the one allowlist here that is
# permissive when empty (FC_ALLOWED_NAMESPACES allowlists nothing when empty).
# The runner logs a `config.image_allowlist_open` warning at startup while it
# is empty, on purpose. Matching is boundary-aware: "ghcr.io/acme" does not
# admit "ghcr.io/acmeattacker/x".
FC_ALLOWED_TOOLCHAIN_IMAGE_PREFIXES=

# Your command allowlist: the bare binary names a test plan's steps may run in
# your cluster, csv. Empty = the runner's built-in default, which is
# FixControl's own closed command set (npm/npx/node/pnpm/yarn/bun, python/pip/
# poetry/pytest/…, composer/php, go/cargo, mvn/gradle/dotnet, bundle/ruby/rake,
# cmake/ctest/meson/ninja/make, wdcli) plus sh and bash. `git` is deliberately
# NOT in it — the checkout lane runs git inside the runner's own image, never
# as a plan step — so add it explicitly if a repository's toolchain needs it.
#
# Why it exists: pinning FixControl's signing key proves WHO authorized a test
# run. It bounds nothing about WHAT that run executes — a plan step's argv goes
# straight into a container command. This list is that bound, and it is yours:
# a step naming a command outside it refuses the operation before a namespace
# is created, and FixControl cannot widen it (a per-operation grant is
# intersected with this list, never added to it).
#
# Narrow it if you can: "npm,npx,node" is a complete value for a Node estate.
# `sh`/`bash` are only ever honoured as "sh <workspace-script>.sh" — an inline
# "-c" program is refused whether or not they are listed.
FC_ALLOWED_COMMANDS=

FC_NAMESPACE_TTL_SECONDS=3600
FC_MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=1

# ── Runner-side checkout (workspaceRef.kind: "git-remote") ─────────────────
# There is deliberately NO value here. The lane is enabled by creating two
# objects this installer does not author, because both are yours: a ConfigMap
# `fc-test-runner-repos` mapping the opaque repoKeys FixControl sends onto your
# clone URLs, and a Secret `fc-test-runner-git` holding the forge credential.
# FixControl never sends a URL or a credential, and a repoKey with no local
# mapping is refused by the runner. Shape, commands and the egress implication:
#
#   install/base/test-runner/customer-git.example.yaml
#
# Leave them uncreated and nothing changes: the runner advertises only
# `channel-tar` and every workspace keeps arriving as a packed tarball.


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  9. Secrets — who owns the Kubernetes Secret objects
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#   1  install.sh creates and updates them from the values above. Simplest, and
#      the right answer for most installs. Material never reaches a command
#      line: it is written to a 0600 file in a private temp directory and
#      passed to kubectl with --from-file, then shredded.
#   0  install.sh does not touch them. You are running External Secrets, Vault,
#      Sealed Secrets or similar, and those own the objects. install.sh then
#      VERIFIES the required keys exist before it deploys anything, and refuses
#      with the missing key names if they do not — see manifests/external-
#      secrets.yaml for the bundle that can own them.
FC_MANAGE_SECRETS=1


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  10. Dev-rig escape hatches — never set these on a real cluster
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# install.sh prints a loud banner for each one it sees.
FC_ALLOW_INSECURE_URL=0
